---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site.ts';
import {
  INVERTER_EFFICIENCY,
  RESERVE_FACTOR,
  USABLE_CAPACITY_FACTOR,
} from '../lib/calculate.ts';

const steps = [
  {
    title: 'Daily energy per device',
    formula: 'watts x hours x duty cycle',
    body: 'Duty cycle is the share of the hour a device actually draws power. A fridge compressor might run 35 percent of the time, so its nameplate wattage overstates its average draw. A router runs continuously, so its duty cycle is 1. The calculator lets you edit all three numbers on every row.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Recommended nameplate capacity',
    formula: '(daily Wh / inverter efficiency) x reserve margin',
    body: 'The inverter converts battery DC to household AC at a loss. Dividing by efficiency makes that loss increase the battery you should shop for, instead of ignoring it. The reserve covers ageing, cold weather, and the gap between a lab figure and a house. Defaults are 0.85 and 1.20. The tool exposes 0.80 / 0.85 / 0.90 and 1.15 / 1.20 / 1.35.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Surge check',
    formula: 'max(single device surge) <= station inverter surge',
    body: 'The check compares only the largest recorded single-device startup surge with the station rating. It does not add other running loads, simultaneous starts, or surge duration. A passing estimate is not confirmation that a device will start. If the station only publishes a resistive boost (X-Boost, Power Lifting, SurgePad), that peak is not used. A known motor surge is then surge_unverified, not cleared.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Continuous output check',
    formula: 'sum(device watts) <= station continuous output',
    body: 'If several devices run at once, the simultaneous draw must stay under the continuous rating. The "if all at once" hours assume every device draws the entered watts with no duty-cycle reduction. This is a comparison estimate, not a guaranteed minimum runtime.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="How PowerStation Size sizes a power station"
  description="Formulas for daily watt-hours, inverter loss, reserve, surge versus boost, solar, and where every station spec is sourced."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-4xl">Methodology</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-500 dark:text-ink-400">Last updated: {site.legalUpdated}.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      Three things decide whether an estimate is useful: the formula, the constants, and where the
      inputs come from. All three are written here so you can disagree with any of them. The same
      functions size the homepage calculator, the scenario pages, and each station's refrigerator
      verdict. If those surfaces ever disagree, that is a bug. Email
      <!--email_off--><a class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300" href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a><!--/email_off-->.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The formulas</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-8">
      {
        steps.map((s) => (
          <div class="border-l-2 border-moss-300 pl-5 dark:border-moss-700">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold">{s.title}</h3>
            <p class="tabular mt-2 rounded-[var(--radius-control)] bg-ink-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm dark:bg-ink-800">
              {s.formula}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-sm text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">{s.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Default constants</h2>
    <dl class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-ink-200 p-4 dark:border-ink-800">
        <dt class="text-sm text-ink-500 dark:text-ink-400">Inverter efficiency</dt>
        <dd class="tabular mt-1 text-2xl font-semibold">{INVERTER_EFFICIENCY.toFixed(2)}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-ink-200 p-4 dark:border-ink-800">
        <dt class="text-sm text-ink-500 dark:text-ink-400">Reserve margin</dt>
        <dd class="tabular mt-1 text-2xl font-semibold">{RESERVE_FACTOR.toFixed(2)}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-ink-200 p-4 dark:border-ink-800">
        <dt class="text-sm text-ink-500 dark:text-ink-400">Usable capacity</dt>
        <dd class="tabular mt-1 text-2xl font-semibold">{USABLE_CAPACITY_FACTOR.toFixed(2)}</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-sm text-ink-500 dark:text-ink-400">
      The runtime calculator multiplies nameplate watt-hours by {USABLE_CAPACITY_FACTOR.toFixed(2)}
      before dividing by average draw. This is a combined planning allowance for usable battery
      energy, conversion losses, and self-consumption, not a measured value for every model or a
      statement about its output voltage. It does not apply the sizing calculator's inverter
      efficiency factor a second time. Actual usable energy varies with the station and load.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Worked example</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      Fridge outage load used on this site: a full-size refrigerator at 150 W for 8 hours at 35
      percent duty (420 Wh), a router at 12 W for 8 hours (96 Wh), an LED lamp at 9 W for 5 hours
      (45 Wh), and a phone at 12 W for 2 hours (24 Wh). Daily energy before conversion is 585 Wh.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      Recommended nameplate is 585 / 0.85 x 1.20, which is 826 Wh. Startup surge on that fridge
      row is 1,200 W. A station whose only peak is a resistive boost is excluded from the match
      list even if its watt-hours are enough. That is the failure this site exists to make
      visible before you buy.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      CPAP without a humidifier is treated as 45 W for 8 hours (360 Wh) plus a phone charge, 384 Wh
      daily, 542 Wh nameplate after loss and reserve. Startup inrush is not modelled in this example;
      check the equipment and adapter requirements. The 80 W humidifier-on entry represents the
      entire CPAP with heating, not an extra 80 W accessory. Choose one configuration per machine,
      not both. These are illustrative inputs, not prescribed therapy settings.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Solar, if you recharge off grid</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      Panel watts needed to replace a day's draw is daily Wh / (peak sun hours x inverter
      efficiency). The default sun figure is 5 hours. The calculator also accepts an array you
      already have and reports how much of the day that array covers, using the same efficiency.
      Panel nameplate is not charge rate. Angle, cloud, season, and heat all reduce output. Treat
      solar as a top-up, not as a way to run a 1,500 W heater indefinitely.
      This simplified energy model reuses the efficiency setting; it does not separately model
      panel, controller, battery, and inverter losses. A wattage match alone does not verify panel
      voltage, current, polarity, connector compatibility, or the station's operating limits.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Where inputs come from</h2>
    <div class="mt-6">
      <div class="border-t border-ink-200 py-5 dark:border-ink-800">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">Station specifications</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[68ch] text-sm text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
          The manufacturer product page. Each model stores the source URL and the date it was
          verified. A reading older than 90 days is flagged on the model page. If the manufacturer
          page and this site disagree, the manufacturer page wins.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t border-ink-200 py-5 dark:border-ink-800">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">Device power ranges</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[68ch] text-sm text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
          Manufacturer documentation where a figure is published, otherwise engineering ranges.
          Each device entry records which it is. You can override watts, hours, and duty cycle
          before you trust a result.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t border-ink-200 py-5 dark:border-ink-800">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">What we do not claim</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-[68ch] text-sm text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
          We do not publish our own runtime test results. Every hour on this site is calculated,
          not measured. We do not rate a station as a medical device. Whole-home backup needs a
          manufacturer-approved, code-compliant transfer or isolation system; this calculator
          does not design that installation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Known limitations</h2>
    <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-sm text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        Missing startup-surge data is treated as zero in the calculation, not as evidence of no
        inrush. The model does not verify waveform, adapter compatibility, automatic transfer
        time, or uninterrupted operation of medical equipment.
      </li>
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        Temperature is not modelled. A lithium battery loses capacity in the cold, and the effect
        is significant below freezing. The 35 percent reserve option is a blunt stand-in, not a
        thermal model.
      </li>
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        Battery ageing is not modelled per unit. Cycle life varies with depth of discharge and heat.
      </li>
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        Device wattages are ranges, not constants. A fridge in a hot garage draws more than the same
        fridge in a cool basement. A CPAP at a high pressure setting draws more than the 45 W or
        80 W seed.
      </li>
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        Dual-voltage stations: continuous watts on this site are the 120 V figure when the
        manufacturer publishes both. A 240 V boost is not treated as 120 V surge.
      </li>
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        Peak sun hours are a planning slider, not a forecast for your roof.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-10 max-w-[68ch] text-sm">
      Use the
      <a class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300" href="/calculator/">calculator</a>
      with these constants, or browse
      <a class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300" href="/stations/">verified station specs</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
